{"id":"https://geo-carto.github.io/vocabularios/pliegue-tipo","type":"ConceptScheme","prefLabel":{"es":"Tipo de pliegue","en":"Fold profile type"},"description":{"es":"Términos que clasifican los pliegues según la geometría cóncava/convexa del pliegue respecto a la superficie terrestre y la relación con la dirección de rejuvenecimiento en los estratos plegados, si se conoce."},"publisher":{"id":"https://www.igme.es/"},"license":{"id":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/"},"hasTopConcept":[{"id":"https://geo-carto.github.io/vocabularios/pliegue-tipo/anticlinal","prefLabel":{"es":"anticlinal","en":"anticline"},"definition":{"es":"Pliegue, generalmente convexo hacia arriba, cuyo núcleo contiene las rocas estratigráficamente más antiguas.","en":"A fold general convex upward, whose core contains the stratigraphically older rocks."}},{"id":"https://geo-carto.github.io/vocabularios/pliegue-tipo/anticlinal-tumbado","prefLabel":{"es":"anticlinal tumbado","en":"overturned anticline"},"altLabel":{"es":["anticlinal volcado (RAC)"]},"definition":{"es":"Pliegue anticlinal con un plano axial inclinado en el que los dos flancos buzan en la misma dirección, presentando, en consecuencia, un flanco inverso.","en":"An asymmetrical anticline with a flank or limb that has been tilted beyond perpendicular so that the beds in that limb are upside-down"}},{"id":"https://geo-carto.github.io/vocabularios/pliegue-tipo/anticlinorio","prefLabel":{"es":"anticlinorio","en":"anticlinorium"},"definition":{"es":"Antiforme o anticlinal regional compuesto por antiformes y sinformes (o anticlinales y sinclinales) más pequeños con espaciamiento sistemático razonable cuya línea media define una estructura antiforme","en":"Regional  antiform or anticline composed of smaller antiforms and synforms (or anticlines and synclines) with reasonable systematic spacing whose midline defines an antiform structure"}},{"id":"https://geo-carto.github.io/vocabularios/pliegue-tipo/antiforma","prefLabel":{"es":"antiforma","en":"antiform"},"definition":{"es":"Pliegue cuyo núcleo contiene las rocas estratigráficamente más modernas; generalmente es cóncavo hacia arriba.","en":"A fold of which the core contains the stratigraphically younger rocks; it is generally concave upward."}},{"id":"https://geo-carto.github.io/vocabularios/pliegue-tipo/cubeta","prefLabel":{"es":"cubeta","en":"basin"},"definition":{"es":"Estructura sinforme hacia cuyo centro buzan las capas.","en":"Synform in which the rock strata are inclined downward from all sides toward the center."}},{"id":"https://geo-carto.github.io/vocabularios/pliegue-tipo/domo","prefLabel":{"es":"domo","en":"dome"},"altLabel":{"es":["cúpula"]},"definition":{"es":"Antiforme con sección horizontal circular o elíptica, y con buzamientos en todas direcciones hacia fuera de la estructura.","en":"Antiform with a circular or elliptical horizontal section in which the rock strata are inclined in all directions towards the outside of the structure."}},{"id":"https://geo-carto.github.io/vocabularios/pliegue-tipo/monoclinal","prefLabel":{"es":"monoclinal","en":"monocline"},"definition":{"es":"Pliegue con un flanco corto y fuerte buzamiento que enlaza dos flancos planos de tendencia horizontal.","en":"Fold with a short, steeply dipping limb that connects two flat limbs with a horizontal trend."}},{"id":"https://geo-carto.github.io/vocabularios/pliegue-tipo/sinclinal","prefLabel":{"es":"sinclinal","en":"syncline"},"definition":{"es":"Pliegue convexo hacia arriba o cóncavo hacia abajo. Este término se utiliza generalmente cuando las capas plegadas no tienen un orden estratigráfico, cuando no se conoce la sucesión estratigráfica de las capas plegadas o cuando el núcleo del pliegue contiene las rocas estratigráficamente más modernas.","en":"Any convex-upward, concave downward fold. The term is usually used when the folded layers do not possess a stratigraphic order, when the stratigraphic order of the folded layers is not known, or when the fold core also contains the stratigraphically younger rock."}},{"id":"https://geo-carto.github.io/vocabularios/pliegue-tipo/sinclinal-tumbado","prefLabel":{"es":"sinclinal tumbado","en":"overturned syncline"},"altLabel":{"es":["sinclinal volcado (RAC)"]},"definition":{"es":"Pliegue sinclinal con un plano axial inclinado en el que los dos flancos buzan en la misma dirección, presentando, en consecuencia, un flanco inverso.","en":"An asymmetrical syncline with a flank or limb that has been tilted beyond perpendicular so that the beds in that limb are upside-down"}},{"id":"https://geo-carto.github.io/vocabularios/pliegue-tipo/sinclinorio","prefLabel":{"es":"sinclinorio","en":"synclinorium"},"definition":{"es":"Sinforme o sinclinal regional compuesto por antiformes y sinformes (o anticlinales y sinclinales) más pequeños con espaciamiento sistemático razonable cuya línea media define una estructura sinforme.","en":"Regional synform or syncline composed of smaller antiforms and synforms (or anticlines and synclines) with reasonable systematic spacing whose midline defines a synform structure"}},{"id":"https://geo-carto.github.io/vocabularios/pliegue-tipo/sinforma","prefLabel":{"es":"sinforma","en":"synform"},"definition":{"es":"Pliegue cuyos flancos se cierran hacia la parte inferior. Este término se utiliza generalmente cuando las capas plegadas no poseen un orden estratigráfico, cuando no se conoce la sucesión estratigráfica de las capas plegadas o cuando el núcleo del pliegue también contiene las rocas estratigráficamente más antiguas.","en":"Any fold whose limbs close at the bottom. The term is usually used when the folded layers do not possess a stratigraphic order, when the stratigraphic order of the folded layers is not known, or when the fold core also contains the stratigraphically older rock."}}],"@context":{"@version":1.1,"id":"@id","type":"@type","@vocab":"http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#","xsd":"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#","dct":"http://purl.org/dc/terms/","dc":"http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/","schema":"https://schema.org/","vann":"http://purl.org/vocab/vann/","ldp":"http://www.w3.org/ns/ldp#","owl":"http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#","title":{"@id":"dct:title","@container":"@language"},"dc:title":{"@id":"dc:title","@container":"@language"},"dc:description":{"@id":"dc:description","@container":"@language"},"description":{"@id":"dct:description","@container":"@language"},"license":{"@id":"dct:license","@container":"@language"},"issued":{"@id":"dct:issued","@type":"xsd:date"},"created":{"@id":"dct:created","@type":"xsd:date"},"modified":{"@id":"dct:modified","@type":"xsd:date"},"creator":"dct:creator","publisher":"dct:publisher","preferredNamespacePrefix":"vann:preferredNamespacePrefix","preferredNamespaceUri":"vann:preferredNamespaceUri","isBasedOn":"schema:isBasedOn","source":"dct:source","prefLabel":{"@container":"@language"},"altLabel":{"@container":["@language","@set"]},"hiddenLabel":{"@container":["@language","@set"]},"definition":{"@container":"@language"},"note":{"@container":["@language","@set"]},"changeNote":{"@container":["@language","@set"]},"editorialNote":{"@container":["@language","@set"]},"historyNote":{"@container":["@language","@set"]},"scopeNote":{"@container":["@language","@set"]},"notation":{"@container":"@set"},"example":{"@container":"@language"},"narrower":{"@container":"@set"},"related":{"@container":"@set"},"relatedMatch":{"@container":"@set"},"narrowerTransitive":{"@container":"@set"},"broaderTransitive":{"@container":"@set"},"broadMatch":{"@container":"@set"},"narrowMatch":{"@container":"@set"},"closeMatch":{"@container":"@set"},"exactMatch":{"@container":"@set"},"hasTopConcept":{"@container":"@set"},"inScheme":{"@container":"@set"},"topConceptOf":{"@container":"@set"},"deprecated":{"@id":"owl:deprecated","@type":"xsd:boolean"},"isReplacedBy":{"@id":"dct:isReplacedBy","@container":"@set"}}}